`ca::report<A>`: an error with its trace, owned, with formatted messages. The thread trace of
layer 3 is free but limited: literal messages only, and it belongs to the thread, so the next
failure replaces it. A report copies it into memory of its own, so it survives being stored,
returned through many layers, or handed to another thread, and its frames can carry formatted
text such as the path that failed.

A function returns `ca::result<T, ca::report<>>` instead of `ca::result<T>`, and adds frames with
`ca::context(error, "reading {}", path)`: from a plain `ca::error` it starts a report with a copy
of the thread trace, from a report it extends it, and in both cases the message is checked at
compile time like any format.

```c++
#include "ca/context.hpp"
#include "ca/error.hpp"
#include "ca/format.hpp"
#include "ca/report.hpp"
#include "ca/result.hpp"
#include "ca/sv.hpp"
#include "ca/trace.hpp"
#include "ca/utility.hpp"

[[nodiscard]] static auto open(const ca::sv::str path) noexcept -> ca::result<int> {
	if (path.starts_with("/etc/"))
		return ca::trace::fail(ca::errc::access_denied, "open");
	return 4;
}

[[nodiscard]] static auto read_config(const ca::sv::str path) noexcept -> ca::result<int, ca::report<>> {
	auto file = open(path);
	if (!file)
		return ca::context(file.error(), "reading {}", path);
	return *file;
}

[[nodiscard]] static auto load(const int attempt) noexcept -> ca::result<int, ca::report<>> {
	auto config = read_config("/etc/app.conf");
	if (!config)
		return ca::context(ca::move(config).error(), "loading settings, attempt {}", attempt);
	return *config;
}

auto main(void) -> int {
	auto r = load(2);
	if (r)
		return 1;
	ca::report<>& report = r.error();
	if (report.error() != ca::errc::access_denied || report.size() != 3U)
		return 2;
	if (report.frame(1).message() != ca::sv::str{"reading /etc/app.conf"})
		return 3;
	auto text = ca::format("{}", report);
	if (!text || !text->view().starts_with("Permission denied\n  at open ("))
		return 4;
	return text->cleanup() && report.cleanup() ? 0 : 5;
}
```

Printed with `{}` or `ca::print_trace`, a report reads like a thread trace:

```text
Permission denied
  at open (config.cpp:12): open
  at read_config (config.cpp:19): reading /etc/app.conf
  at load (config.cpp:26): loading settings, attempt 2
```

A report holds the error, one string with every message end to end, and an array of frames
(offset, size and source location): two allocations whatever the number of frames, none while
the messages fit inline. All of it is paid on the failure path only. Frames that cannot be stored
for lack of memory are counted in `dropped()` rather than failing the error path itself.

`ca::report<>::of(error)` builds one by hand, and `report.context("...", args...)` adds a frame in
place. A context can fail with a report (`ca::with<ca::report<>>(enter, leave)`), which turns a
trap into a report carrying the trap's reason. Clean a report up with `cleanup()`.
